ABSTRACT

This dissertation proposes, formally defines, and mathematically validates a novel global infrastructure: the Global AI Risk Radar (GARR) — a continuously learning, cryptographically privacy‑preserving, and architecturally layered monitoring system designed to detect emerging existential threats across biological, artificial intelligence, and engineered critical systems before they escalate beyond recoverable thresholds. Drawing on four bodies of empirical evidence — the 2025 Wittmann et al. demonstration that AI‑designed proteins systematically evade homology‑based DNA synthesis screening; Anthropic’s frontier red team documentation of Claude’s virology benchmarks exceeding expert‑level performance within a single year; the forensically documented institutional collapse of the Global Public Health Intelligence Network (GPHIN) in May 2019; and SC Media’s 2026 projection that over one‑third of global energy and utilities infrastructure has already experienced adversarial cyber pre‑positioning — I argue that existing early‑warning systems are structurally incapable of detecting convergence risks: threat scenarios in which AI‑enabled biological design, DNA synthesis, and infrastructure compromise combine into a single autonomous threat vector. The dissertation makes four original contributions: (1) a unified theoretical architecture for GARR across four interdependent layers, with rigorous mathematical formalization of Layer 4 (dynamic Bayesian signal integration) and Layer 1 (functional manifold screening); (2) a formal stochastic safety condition derived via Itô calculus and barrier certificates; (3) a governance model achieving global function without prior political consensus, grounded in cryptographic privacy and minimax robustness; (4) a game‑theoretic proof of adversarial evasion bounds under incomplete information. This is a theoretical dissertation; engineering implementation and empirical validation are designated as follow‑on research.

Keywords: existential risk surveillance, biosecurity, AI capability monitoring, convergence threats, critical infrastructure protection, DNA synthesis screening, stochastic control, Bayesian change‑point detection, game‑theoretic robustness

