CHAPTER 2: LITERATURE REVIEW — FRAGMENTS OF A NERVOUS SYSTEM

2.1 The Architecture of Incompleteness

The scientific and policy literature on existential risk surveillance presents a striking pattern: sophisticated partial systems exist in each relevant domain, each making genuine progress, yet none speak to each other in real time. This chapter reviews the three principal domains — biological surveillance, AI capability monitoring, and critical infrastructure threat detection — and documents both achievements and structural gaps.

2.2 Biological Surveillance: The Sequence‑Function Gap

The Global Public Health Intelligence Network (GPHIN) stands as the canonical case study. Created in 1994, GPHIN supplied the World Health Organization with 20 % of its epidemiological intelligence at its peak. By 2018, its alerts had fallen from 877 annually to 21. The alert system went entirely silent on May 24, 2019, after a departmental edict required management approval for every warning. Analysts were reassigned to domestic projects. On December 31, 2019, GPHIN logged the Wuhan pneumonia notice but issued no public alert. The lesson: surveillance infrastructure that depends on continuous political will to function has a known failure mode.

At the technical layer, current DNA synthesis screening covers approximately 80 % of global synthesis orders through the International Gene Synthesis Consortium’s voluntary protocol. However, screening relies on homology‑based alignment (e.g., BLAST scores). The Wittmann et al. (2025) study demonstrated that open‑source protein design models (ProteinMPNN, EvoDiff‑MSA, EvoDiff‑Seq) could generate hazardous functional variants with little or no sequence similarity to known threats. Detection rates on commercial platforms dropped as low as 23 % for some variants.

The most advanced response is SecureDNA, a free, privacy‑preserving, fully automated system capable of verifiably screening all synthesis orders of ≥30 nucleotides against an up‑to‑date database of controlled sequences. Its cryptographic architecture — a Distributed Oblivious Pseudorandom Function — ensures that neither the hazard database nor customer sequences are exposed. SecureDNA represents the closest existing approximation to GARR’s bio layer, but it does not yet implement function‑based screening, and it remains voluntary.

2.3 AI Capability Monitoring: The Evaluation Gap

Anthropic activated AI Safety Level 3 safeguards for chemical and biological threats in May 2025, proving that unilateral implementation was feasible. The UK AI Security Institute’s Inspect framework, released in May 2024, has grown to 50+ contributors and ships 200+ pre‑built evaluations. Despite this progress, the International AI Safety Report 2026 identifies an evaluation gap: performance on pre‑deployment tests does not reliably predict real‑world utility or risk. Developers have incentives to keep information proprietary, and the pace of development creates pressure to prioritize speed over risk management.

AI‑related safety incidents numbered 233 in 2024 and 362 in 2025 — a 55 % annual increase. No current system continuously monitors for phase transitions (sudden capability jumps) across frontier models from multiple labs.

2.4 Critical Infrastructure: Pre‑Positioning as the New Threat Paradigm

By 2026, the most dangerous cyber events will not look like attacks at all. They will look like reasonable, automated decisions made at scale — until systems fail. A single compromised or poorly governed AI agent in energy or logistics will trigger automated responses across tightly coupled systems. Legacy industrial control systems remain hard to patch, poorly segmented, and difficult to monitor. The current defensive posture — AI‑powered anomaly detection on OT networks — is reactive by architecture: it looks for deviations from known patterns. Pre‑positioned adversaries who understand the detection baseline can operate below it.

2.5 The Gap This Dissertation Addresses

No peer‑reviewed theoretical framework currently exists for a unified signal that integrates bio, AI, and infrastructure surveillance into a single coherent threat probability estimate. This dissertation constructs that framework, with formal mathematical foundations.

