CHAPTER 7: ADVERSARIAL ROBUSTNESS — GAME‑THEORETIC AND INFORMATION‑THEORETIC BOUNDS

7.1 Stackelberg Game with Incomplete Information

We model the interaction between GARR (Defender $\mathcal{D}$) and a malicious actor (Adversary $\mathcal{A}$). $\mathcal{D}$ chooses a configuration of hidden classification weights and evaluation benchmarks $\boldsymbol{\omega} \in \Omega$, kept private within secure enclaves. $\mathcal{A}$ attempts to synthesize an evasive biological sequence or exploit a frontier model capability by selecting an optimization vector $s \in \mathcal{A}^L$. $\mathcal{A}$ does not see $\boldsymbol{\omega}$ directly but can query the system or observe public outputs, receiving noisy signals $y \in \mathcal{Y}$ according to $p(y \mid s, \boldsymbol{\omega})$.

7.2 Minimax Objective

Define the loss function $\mathcal{L}(s, \boldsymbol{\omega})$ as the joint penalty for the adversary’s success minus detection penalty plus cost:

\mathcal{L}(s, \boldsymbol{\omega}) = \Psi(\mathbf{F}(\phi(s))) - \log P_{\text{conv}}(s; \boldsymbol{\omega}) + c_{\mathcal{A}}(s)

where $\Psi(\cdot)$ is the true lethality (upper‑bounded by 1), $P_{\text{conv}}(s; \boldsymbol{\omega})$ is GARR’s detection probability, and $c_{\mathcal{A}}(s)$ is the adversary’s cost. The defender solves the distributionally robust minimax problem:

\min_{\mathbb{P}_{\boldsymbol{\omega}} \in \mathcal{P}} \max_{s \in \mathcal{A}^L} \mathbb{E}_{\boldsymbol{\omega} \sim \mathbb{P}_{\boldsymbol{\omega}}} \left[ \mathcal{L}(s, \boldsymbol{\omega}) \right]

where $\mathcal{P}$ is an ambiguity set of probability distributions over $\Omega$ (constraining the defender’s randomization).

7.3 Information‑Theoretic Evasion Bound

Theorem 2 (Evasion Gradient Bound). Let the defender’s strategy distribution $\mathbb{P}_{\boldsymbol{\omega}}$ satisfy an entropy lower bound $H(\mathbb{P}_{\boldsymbol{\omega}}) \ge \mathcal{H}_{\text{min}} > 0$. For any first‑order black‑box optimization algorithm employed by the adversary (e.g., stochastic gradient descent on an estimated gradient $\nabla_s \mathcal{L}$), the expected squared norm of the gradient estimation error after $K$ probes is bounded below by:

\mathbb{E}\left[ \|\hat{\nabla}_s \mathcal{L} - \nabla_s \mathcal{L}\|^2 \right] \ge \frac{\mathcal{H}_{\text{min}}^2}{K \cdot \mathcal{I}(s; y)}

where $\mathcal{I}(s; y)$ is the mutual information leaked by GARR’s public alert signals. Consequently, as $K \to \infty$, the variance of the adversary’s gradient estimate does not vanish if $\mathcal{H}_{\text{min}} > 0$ and $\mathcal{I}(s; y)$ is finite — the adversary cannot obtain a consistent estimate of the true gradient.

Proof sketch. By the Cramér‑Rao lower bound for biased estimators under a random parameter $\boldsymbol{\omega}$, the Fisher information matrix of the observation model $p(y \mid s, \boldsymbol{\omega})$ is bounded above by the mutual information $\mathcal{I}(s; y)$. The entropy $H(\mathbb{P}_{\boldsymbol{\omega}})$ lower bounds the variance of any unbiased estimator of $\boldsymbol{\omega}$ via the van Trees inequality. Transforming this to the gradient space using the chain rule for Fisher information yields the result. (Full derivation in Appendix B.)

Corollary. If the defender maintains minimum entropy $\mathcal{H}_{\text{min}} > 0$ by regularly rotating evaluation benchmarks, perturbing classifier thresholds, and adding controlled noise to outputs, then the probability that the adversary finds an evasive sequence $s$ with $\mathcal{L}(s, \boldsymbol{\omega}) < \epsilon$ within a finite time horizon $T$ goes to zero as $\mathcal{H}_{\text{min}}$ increases.

Thus, secrecy of baseline parameters + algorithmic randomization (moving‑target defense) is mathematically sufficient to neutralize automated AI design agents trying to optimize around GARR’s filters.

