CHAPTER 8: LIMITATIONS AND FUTURE WORK

8.1 False Positive Desensitization

If $P_{\text{conv}}(t)$ crosses YELLOW too often, human responders develop alert fatigue. GARR’s threshold calibration must prioritize precision over recall at RED level. The calibration procedure uses Bayesian updating: each alert’s outcome informs next cycle’s thresholds.

8.2 Adversarial Exploitation of L4

An adversary could attempt to poison L4’s Bayesian network by feeding false $X_{\text{bio}}$ signals, triggering YELLOW/RED alerts at will (cry‑wolf attack) or suppressing real signals (invisible attack). Mitigation requires that all inputs to L4 be cryptographically authenticated and source‑attributed, with anomaly scores computed independently by each layer using private data. This is assumed in the governance model.

8.3 The Last‑Mile Problem

Early, credible warning is necessary but not sufficient for effective response. GARR does not solve the institutional failure mode where governments ignore alerts. This is a political science problem beyond this dissertation’s scope.

8.4 Future Research Agenda

