APPENDIX C: DERIVATION OF THE BAYESIAN CHANGE‑POINT RECURSION (LAYER 2)

The filtered odds ratio for phase‑transition detection is derived as follows. Let $Y_{1:t} = (Y_1, \dots, Y_t)$ be the sequence of benchmark scores. Define the posterior probability of a change point at time $t$ as

\pi_t = \mathbb{P}(\rho(t)=1 \mid Y_{1:t}).

Assume that if no change occurs, the parameters $(\mu, \Sigma)$ remain constant; if a change occurs, new parameters are drawn from a prior distribution $G$. The standard Bayesian change‑point detection algorithm (e.g., Fearnhead, 2006) computes the predictive probability:

Let $H_t$ be the hypothesis that the last change occurred at time $t$ (so the current run length $r_t = t - t_{\text{last}}$). Then

\pi_t = \frac{p(Y_t \mid \text{new epoch}) \cdot \mathbb{P}(\rho(t)=1 \mid Y_{1:t-1})}{p(Y_t \mid Y_{1:t-1})}.

The Bayes factor $\Lambda(t)$ is exactly the ratio of the predictive likelihood under a new epoch to that under the current epoch. For a Gaussian model with conjugate prior, this ratio can be computed in closed form (see Appendix D). The recursion is:

\Lambda(t) = \frac{\int p(Y_t \mid \theta) dG(\theta)}{\int p(Y_t \mid \mu_{t-1}, \Sigma_{t-1}) d\Pi(\mu_{t-1}, \Sigma_{t-1} \mid Y_{1:t-1})},

where $\Pi$ is the posterior distribution of parameters for the current run. The filtered odds are then updated via

\pi_t = \frac{\Lambda(t) \pi_{t-1}}{1 - \pi_{t-1} + \Lambda(t) \pi_{t-1}}.

For computational tractability, we use a particle filter or the BayesCP algorithm (Fearnhead & Liu, 2007). In practice, the prior probability of a change is set proportional to the increase in cumulative compute: $\mathbb{P}(\rho(t)=1) \propto \log(\text{FLOPs}(t) / \text{FLOPs}(t-1))$.

